# Technical Architecture: Rent & Lease Management System

As the Technical Architect for this project, I have translated our high-level System Architecture into a detailed, "ground-level" implementation plan. While the System Architecture defined *what* we are building, this **Technical Architecture** dictates exactly *how* we are building it using our specific technology stack.

Per your instructions, this document contains **no diagrams** and focuses purely on the physical and digital infrastructure required to bring the system to life.

---

## 1. Core Components (The Software & Hardware Stack)

This section acts as the engineering blueprint, detailing the exact technologies, frameworks, and protocols our developers will use to implement the system.

### A. Software Stack
*   **Backend Framework (Core Logic):** **Python 3.11+** running the **Django 4.x** web framework. Django is chosen for its "batteries-included" approach, providing built-in authentication, ORM (Object-Relational Mapping), and robust middleware routing.
*   **API Protocol:** **Django REST Framework (DRF)** will be used to serialize backend data into JSON format for the frontend to consume.
*   **Real-Time Middleware:** **Django Channels** running on an ASGI (Asynchronous Server Gateway Interface) server like Daphne or Uvicorn. This upgrades standard Django from synchronous to asynchronous, enabling the chat feature.
*   **Frontend Technologies:** **HTML5** for semantic structuring, **CSS3** (using a strict 4px base-unit design system) for styling, and **Vanilla JavaScript (ES6+)** for DOM manipulation and API fetching.
*   **Database Management System:** **MySQL 8.0**. We will specifically utilize the **InnoDB storage engine** because it natively supports the ACID transactions required for processing rent payments safely.
*   **Message Broker (Required for Chat):** **Redis**. Django Channels requires an in-memory data store like Redis to route real-time WebSocket messages between different users efficiently.

### B. Hardware Infrastructure
*   **Hosting Environment:** A Linux-based cloud virtual machine (e.g., Ubuntu 22.04 on AWS EC2 or DigitalOcean Droplet).
*   **Storage:** SSD-based block storage for the MySQL database to ensure fast read/write speeds for financial queries, plus an S3-compatible cloud bucket for storing static assets (like tenant lease PDFs and chat image attachments).

### C. Network & Connectivity
*   **Web Traffic (Synchronous):** All standard web traffic (dashboards, forms, payments) will be routed over **HTTPS (Port 443)** using TLS 1.2+ encryption.
*   **Chat Traffic (Asynchronous):** All real-time chat data will be routed over **WSS (WebSocket Secure)** protocols.
*   **Firewall Rules:** The MySQL database port (3306) and Redis port (6379) will be strictly blocked from public internet access, allowing communication only internally from the Django application server.

### D. Integration Architecture
*   **Frontend-to-Backend:** The Vanilla JavaScript frontend will communicate with Django via the **Fetch API**, using asynchronous `Promises` to send JSON payloads.
*   **Payment Integration:** Integration with external financial systems (e.g., Stripe or Paystack API) will be handled securely via backend-to-backend Webhooks, ensuring the client-side JavaScript never handles sensitive transaction processing.

